Historical waves of automation — the mechanisation of agriculture, the industrial revolution, the computerisation of offices — all caused temporary displacement but ultimately created more and better jobs than they eliminated.
AI tools are disproportionately boosting the productivity of less-skilled workers: a 2023 MIT/Stanford study found AI assistants improved novice programmers' output by 40% but experienced programmers' by only 8%.
The OECD projects that while AI will change 60% of jobs, fewer than 15% of jobs face a high risk of full automation — most workers will be augmented, not replaced.
Countries that lead in AI adoption (US, China, UK) are also experiencing the highest productivity growth, consistent with AI driving economic gains.
Clean energy AI — optimising grid dispatch, accelerating materials discovery for batteries — could reduce the cost of the clean energy transition by $1–2 trillion, making climate action more economically viable.
